original_question:
Find $a+b+c$, given that $x+y\neq -1$ and  \begin{align*}
ax+by+c&=x+7,\\
a+bx+cy&=2x+6y,\\
ay+b+cx&=4x+y.
\end{align*}

original_answer:
Since these equations look symmetric, at least on the left-hand side, we add all three together.  This gives us  \begin{align*}
&a(x+1+y)+b(y+x+1)+c(1+y+x)\\
&\qquad\qquad=x+7+2x+6y+4x+y.
\end{align*} We then factor out $(1+x+y)$ on the left and simplify the right side to get \begin{align*}
(1+x+y)(a+b+c)&=7x+7y+7, \text{ so}\\
(1+x+y)(a+b+c)&=7(1+x+y).
\end{align*}

Since $x+y\neq -1$, we know $x+y+1\neq 0$, so we can divide both sides by that term and find that $a+b+c=\boxed{7}$.

In addition to knowing how to use mixed numbers, to solve this problem one also must recall two of the basic properties of exponents: \[a^b \cdot a^c = a^{b+c}\] and \[\left(l^m\right)^n = l^{m \cdot n}.\] Keeping these properties in mind, we can proceed with simplification \begin{align*}
\left(2^{\left(1\frac{1}{4}\right)}\right)^{\frac{2}{5}} \cdot \left(4^{\left(3\frac{1}{8}\right)}\right)^{\frac{2}{25}}
&= \left(2^{\frac{5}{4}}\right)^{\frac{2}{5}} \cdot \left(4^{\frac{25}{8}}\right)^{\frac{2}{25}}\\
&= 2^{\frac{5}{4} \cdot \frac{2}{5}} \cdot 4^{\frac{25}{8} \cdot \frac{2}{25}}\\
&= 2^{\frac{2}{4}} \cdot (2^2)^{\frac{2}{8}}\\
&= 2^{\frac{1}{2}} \cdot 2^{2 \cdot \frac{1}{4}}\\
&= 2^{\frac{1}{2}} \cdot 2^{\frac{1}{2}}\\
&= 2^{(\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2})}\\
&= \boxed{2}.
\end{align*}

Using the extended Sine law, we find the circumradius of $ABC$ to be $R = \frac{BC}{2\sin A} = 4\sqrt 2$.

